add(777,'learning-motivation','Choose the role of learning in an unfamiliar environment','A robot must recognize many unfamiliar objects under changing appearances; why may learning from representative data be preferable to enumerating every appearance rule?', ['It can learn useful regularities across examples rather than requiring a separate hand-written rule for every variation','It guarantees correct recognition of every object regardless of the training distribution','It removes the need to define a task, collect data or evaluate errors','It makes all geometric models and safety checks unnecessary'],0,'Learning can reduce the need to enumerate complex variations, but its generalization and safety still require appropriate data and evaluation.',['This is the motivation stated in the lecture.','Unseen conditions can still cause errors.','Task definition, data and evaluation remain necessary.','Learning does not eliminate other useful models or safety measures.'],typ='scenario',assumptions=['The training examples are relevant to the intended recognition task.'])
add(778,'expert-system-rules','Identify an expert-system rule representation','Which approach matches the lecture’s rule-based expert-system example?', ['An engineer writes if-then-else conditions using domain knowledge','A classifier receives labeled examples and finds a nearest training neighbor for each query','A model groups unlabeled observations by a fitted similarity measure','An agent updates an action policy from interaction rewards'],0,'The illustrated expert system directly encodes human knowledge as explicit decision rules.',['This is the specified rule-authoring process.','This describes the later supervised nearest-neighbor example.','This is an unlabeled-learning approach rather than the illustrated explicit rules.','This describes reward-driven learning rather than a manually authored blacklist.'])
add([778,780],'rule-system-limitations','Distinguish knowledge acquisition from execution speed','Experts can recognize a defect but cannot express reliable pixel-level conditions for it; which cited limitation is most directly illustrated?', ['Difficulty translating human recognition knowledge into executable rules','The rule engine necessarily runs slower than every trained model','The task has no possible numeric data representation','Every existing rule becomes false when a new image is received'],0,'The lecture distinguishes human holistic understanding from explicit machine-level representations and rule authoring.',['This directly matches the described knowledge/representation mismatch.','No universal speed comparison follows.','Images can be represented numerically even when rules are hard to write.','New input does not automatically invalidate every rule.'],typ='scenario')
add(780,'domain-rule-maintenance','Qualify the domain-change rewrite claim','A spam filter gains a new type of unwanted message; what can reasonably follow for its manually authored rules?', ['Some rules may need additions or revisions; a complete rewrite is not logically required by every domain change','Every existing rule must be deleted for every new message pattern','No rule can ever be reused outside the exact original sample messages','A new pattern automatically changes the blacklist without any learning or editing mechanism'],0,'Domain changes can require maintenance, but the source’s statement that every change needs an entire rewrite is too absolute.',['This states the maintenance risk without assuming all rules are unusable.','Some existing rules may remain useful.','Rules can cover multiple inputs and may be reusable.','The simple fixed blacklist has no automatic update mechanism.'],conflict='Page780 says any task/domain change requires the entire rule set to be rewritten; this is an overstatement rather than a universal property.')
