add(779,'spam-code-matching','Trace case-insensitive blacklist matching','Using the printed blacklist filter, what happens to the message "You have won a FREE MONEY prize! Click here now."?', ['SPAM with trigger free money','SPAM with trigger click here','NOT SPAM because FREE MONEY is uppercase','SPAM with trigger win prize'],0,'The message is lowercased and free money is the first matching blacklist entry; the function returns before testing later matches.',['This follows both lowercasing and list order.','click here also matches, but a previous match already returned.','Lowercasing removes that case difference.','The phrase win prize is not the literal substring used here.'],typ='code_output',assumptions=[black])
add(779,'spam-code-matching','Separate list order from textual order','The message is "urgent: click here for free money"; which trigger is returned by the printed filter?', ['free money','urgent','click here','All three triggers in one returned list'],0,'The loop follows blacklist order, not the position where a phrase first appears in the message, and returns one string at the first match.',['This is the earliest matching list entry.','This is first in the message but last in the blacklist.','An earlier blacklist entry already matches.','The function returns immediately instead of accumulating matches.'],typ='code_output',assumptions=[black])
add(779,'spam-code-substrings','Distinguish substrings from whole words','What does the printed filter return for the lowercase text "insurgent"?', ['SPAM with trigger urgent','NOT SPAM because urgent is not a separate word','SPAM with trigger win prize','An error because no spaces occur'],0,'Python substring membership finds urgent inside insurgent; the code does not tokenize words or require word boundaries.',['This follows the literal in operation.','The code does not check complete-word boundaries.','That phrase is absent.','A string without spaces is still a valid input.'],typ='code_output',assumptions=[black])
add(779,'spam-code-false-negatives','Explain a missed phrase variant','Why does the printed filter label "Get your FREE product today at no cost." as NOT SPAM?', ['None of the exact listed substrings occurs, although the slide presents it as a missed spam variant','The word FREE is always ignored because uppercase text cannot be converted','The function stops before checking any blacklist entry','Every message containing product is explicitly whitelisted'],0,'The blacklist contains free money, not the isolated word free, and it contains no no-cost phrase.',['This explains the literal result without claiming semantic understanding.','The code lowercases the whole text.','The loop does check the list.','No product whitelist exists.'],typ='code_output',assumptions=[black])
add(779,'spam-code-formatting','Read the output formatting independently of classification','In print(f"{spam_filter(e):<20} | {e[:55]}"), what do :<20 and e[:55] do?', ['Left-align the classification in a minimum20-character field and print at most the first55 message characters','Truncate every classification to20characters and print the final55 message characters','Right-align the classification and delete the first55 message characters','Change the classifier threshold to20 and use55 nearest neighbors'],0,'The format width is a minimum padding width, while the string slice selects characters from the start up to but excluding index55.',['This matches both formatting operations.','Field width does not truncate a longer string, and the slice starts at the beginning.','The alignment direction and slice interpretation are wrong.','These are output-formatting expressions, not model parameters.'],typ='code',conflict='Some rendered output prose on page779 is longer than the literal e[:55] slice; distinguish illustrative source display from exact program output.')
add(781,'grayscale-representation','Interpret the pixel representation','Under the slide’s8-bit grayscale convention, what do intensity0 and255 represent?', ['Black and white respectively','White and black respectively','Two different digit class labels','The number of rows and columns in every image'],0,'The displayed legend maps0 to black and255 to white; these are intensity values, not object labels.',['This matches the visible legend.','This reverses the convention.','Pixel values are features, not the image’s semantic class.','The image dimensions are8×8, independent of those endpoint intensities.'],typ='diagram',assumptions=['Use the declared8-bit grayscale encoding; other image formats can use different bit depths.'])
add(781,'grayscale-representation','Count unrestricted8-bit image arrays','An8×8 grayscale image permits256 values independently at each pixel; how many distinct arrays are possible?', ['256^64=2^512','256×64','64^256','2^64'],0,'There are64 pixels and256 choices at each position, so the multiplication rule gives256^64.',['This counts independent choices at all64positions.','Multiplication by64 counts choices for only one selected pixel, not every array.','This swaps the number of positions and choices.','This would allow only two values per pixel.'],typ='numerical',steps=['Compute8×8=64pixels.','Multiply256 choices64times.','Use256=2^8 to obtain2^(8×64).'],assumptions=['Count all arrays, not only plausible digit images; impose no constraints between pixels.'])
add(781,'pixel-array-shape','Flatten the shown image consistently','If the displayed8×8 grid is flattened row by row, what are its vector length and first four values?', ['Length64; values[0,0,180,220]','Length8; values[0,0,180,220]','Length64; values[0,0,0,0]','Length256; values[180,220,220,180]'],0,'Flattening retains all64cells, and the actual top row starts0,0,180,220.',['This uses the visible first row and total cell count.','This confuses rows with total scalar entries.','This does not follow the specified first row.','The intensity-level count is not the vector length.'],typ='diagram',assumptions=['Use row-major order and the displayed numeric grid.'])
add(782,'learning-generalization','Distinguish learned regularities from guaranteed prediction','What is the intended role of a learned model on new unseen data?', ['Use patterns learned from training examples to make predictions, whose accuracy still needs evaluation','Return the true answer by definition whenever a query was absent from training','Replace every feature vector with a manually authored if-then rule','Declare every unseen sample invalid because only training samples can be predicted'],0,'Generalization is the goal of learning, not a guarantee that every future prediction is correct.',['This states the intended use and limitation.','Unseen-data correctness is not guaranteed by the definition.','That is not how every learned model operates.','Models are specifically used to predict new inputs.'])
add(782,'iris-training-test','Separate training from held-out evaluation','Why does the Iris code fit on X_train,y_train and score on X_test,y_test?', ['To evaluate predictions on examples that were not used by that fit call','To provide test labels as extra training features before fitting','To guarantee that future robots have exactly the same accuracy','To make the nearest-neighbor classifier ignore the stored training labels'],0,'The held-out set provides a separate evaluation of this fitted model; it is not a universal guarantee.',['This is the train/test distinction.','The code does not append test labels as features.','A particular held-out score does not prove identical future performance.','Nearest-neighbor classification uses the training labels.'],typ='code')
add(782,'iris-repeatability','Interpret the fixed split seed','What is the role of random_state=0 in the displayed train_test_split call?', ['It makes the randomized split reproducible under the same data and software behavior','It forces all class labels to zero','It sets the number of neighbors to zero','It guarantees the highest possible accuracy among all splits'],0,'The parameter controls pseudorandom splitting, not targets, neighbor count or model optimality.',['This is the reproducibility role.','It does not rewrite the labels.','n_neighbors is a different estimator parameter.','A fixed seed does not optimize the score.'],typ='code')
add(782,'iris-accuracy-rounding','Interpret the displayed97percent result','In a reproduction of the exact1NN snippet,37 of38 held-out samples are correct; what does formatting the score with .0% produce?', ['97%','37%','0%','100%'],0,'The score is37/38≈0.973684; percentage formatting multiplies by100 and rounds to zero decimal places.',['This matches the printed source output.','Thirty-seven is the number correct, not the percentage.','The fraction is not rounded as an ordinary zero-decimal number before percentage conversion.','One error prevents100percent accuracy.'],typ='numerical',steps=['Divide37 by38.','Convert to97.3684percent.','Round to an integer percent.'],assumptions=['The stated37/38 count is supplied from the reproducible installed-version check, not inferred solely from the rounded97percent.'])
add(782,'iris-score-not-predict','Read the actual final operation in the Iris snippet','Although the caption lists load,split,train,predict, which operation does the displayed final line explicitly call?', ['model.score(X_test,y_test) to report accuracy','model.predict(X_test) and print every predicted class','model.fit(X_test,y_test) to retrain on test labels','train_test_split again to optimize the seed'],0,'The displayed print line calls score and formats the scalar result; it does not print an explicit predict-call output.',['This matches the code.','Prediction is involved in scoring conceptually, but this is not the printed call.','The final line does not fit again.','No second split occurs in that line.'],typ='code')
