add(792,'terrain-evaluation-limit','Distinguish a prediction demonstration from accuracy testing','The example predicts a terrain label for one unlabeled new query;what has this demonstrated?', ['The fitted classifier produces that prediction,but no measured test accuracy follows without a known target','The classifier has100percent accuracy because it returned a label','All future terrain conditions will be classified correctly','The six training examples have become an independent held-out test set'],0,'A prediction can be computed without its true label,but correctness or test accuracy cannot be assessed from the prediction alone.',['This distinguishes inference from labeled evaluation.','Producing an output does not establish correctness.','The toy example does not validate every future condition.','The same data used to fit are not independent held-out data.'],typ='scenario')
add([791,793],'feature-scale-sensitivity','Explain nearly horizontal terrain boundaries','The unscaled speed values are around1 while vibration values span10to65;why can the displayed decision regions be dominated by vibration?', ['Raw Euclidean distances are sensitive to feature scale,so large numeric frequency differences can outweigh speed differences','The classifier ignores the first column by definition whenever there are two features','Hertz and meters per second are automatically standardized by NumPy arrays','The class labels are sorted by vibration and therefore force exactly horizontal boundaries for every query'],0,'The code shows no normalization or scaling;Euclidean distance compares the supplied numeric coordinate differences.',['This explains the geometry without claiming speed is completely unused.','Both feature columns are included by the estimator.','An array does not automatically standardize unlike feature units.','The labels are category IDs,and the exact boundaries depend on both coordinates.'],typ='diagram',assumptions=['Interpret the actual unscaled code,not an unstated preprocessing pipeline.'])
add(793,'terrain-plot-palette','Resolve the text-name versus plotted-color discrepancy','The source names Gravel(Red),but its plotted upper region is purple while the low smooth-floor region looks red;what should determine the class interpretation?', ['The numeric label mapping and the stated region names,with the palette discrepancy noted','The color word alone,so every red plotted region must be gravel regardless of labels','The vertical axis must be class ID because region colors differ','The classifier output must be changed from1to0to match a red region'],0,'The source’s class mapping and predicted label are explicit;the palette presentation is inconsistent and should not silently redefine them.',['This preserves data semantics and records the display issue.','A mismatched palette does not change the label dictionary.','The axis is vibration frequency,not class ID.','A color discrepancy does not justify altering the numerical prediction.'],typ='diagram',conflict='Page793’s plotted colors do not visibly match the color names stored in terrain_names;class IDs and named region descriptions are authoritative for this example.')
add(791,'python-import-roles','Match the terrain-code imports to their roles','Which role assignment matches the shown imports?', ['NumPy builds numeric arrays;Matplotlib supports plotting;KNeighborsClassifier supplies the estimator','NumPy assigns terrain labels automatically;Matplotlib fits nearest neighbors;KNeighborsClassifier draws every axis','ListedColormap computes Euclidean distances;NumPy performs cloud uploads;Matplotlib stores training labels','KNeighborsClassifier is a NumPy array constructor;ListedColormap selects the number of neighbors'],0,'The imports separate numeric data representation,visualization and the classification model.',['This matches the packages and symbols used.','The manually supplied labels and estimator roles are misplaced.','These are not the roles of the imported objects.','The estimator and colormap are not interchangeable with arrays or neighbor-count settings.'],typ='code')
add(794,'linear-model-parameters','Distinguish model parameters from input features','In y_hat=w0*x0+w1*x1+b,which quantities are learned parameters rather than a particular sample’s input features?', ['w0,w1 and b','x0 and x1 only','y_hat and the sample row number','The names of the target classes only'],0,'The weights and intercept are fitted model parameters;each sample supplies the feature values used in the prediction.',['This matches the displayed model.','These are the sample features.','The prediction is an output,not the parameter vector.','The illustrated linear regression predicts a numeric value,not class-name parameters.'])
add(794,'linear-model-prediction','Evaluate a two-feature affine prediction','For w=[2,−1],x=[3,4]and b=0.5,what is y_hat=w·x+b?', ['2.5','1.5','10.5','−1.5'],0,'The weighted sum is2×3−1×4=2;adding the intercept gives2.5.',['This uses both signs and the intercept.','This subtracts rather than adds the intercept.','This changes the negative weight to positive.','This reverses the two weighted contributions and then adds the intercept.'],typ='numerical',steps=['Multiply2×3=6 and−1×4=−4.','Add the products to2.','Add0.5.'],formulas=[formula('y_hat=Σ_j w_j x_j+b',{'y_hat':'predicted numeric target','x_j':'feature j','w_j':'weight for feature j','b':'intercept','j':'feature index'},{'y_hat,b':'target units','x_j':'feature-j units','w_j':'target units per feature-j unit','j':'dimensionless'})])
add(794,'linear-indexing-count','Correct the feature-index count explicitly','The slide writes x[0]through x[p]but calls this p features;with zero-based contiguous indexing,which statement is correct?', ['Indices0throughp containp+1features;exactlyp features use0throughp−1','Indices0throughp containp features because zero does not count','Exactlyp features require indices1throughp+1','The intercept b is always the feature x[p],so no separate count is needed'],0,'Inclusive zero-based indexing from0to p hasp+1entries;the source’s label and displayed final index differ by one.',['This resolves the off-by-one convention explicitly.','Index0is a valid entry and must be counted.','That range containsp+1entries as well.','The formula writes the intercept separately from the features.'],conflict='Page794 labels a formula ending atx[p]as aP-feature model;the inclusive zero-based range actually hasp+1entries.')
add(794,'ols-scope','Distinguish a model family from a fitting criterion','How should the slide’s statement that linear regression is also known as OLS be qualified?', ['Ordinary least squares is one fitting criterion for a linear model,not a synonym for every linear-regression method','Every linear model must use an unregularized squared-error fit','Ridge cannot be a linear-regression method because it differs from OLS','OLS describes only the number of input features,not the fitting objective'],0,'The lecture itself lists Ridge;linear prediction forms can be fitted with different objectives or constraints,while OLS specifically minimizes ordinary squared residuals.',['This distinguishes model form and estimator.','Regularized alternatives exist.','Ridge retains a linear prediction form.','OLS refers to the estimation criterion rather than feature count.'],conflict='Page794 presents linear regression andOLS as interchangeable;retainOLS as the particular ordinary squared-error fitting method.')
add(795,'squared-error-mean','Distinguish mean squared error from its sum','For true targets[1,3,5]and predictions[2,2,4],what are SSE and MSE?', ['SSE=3 and MSE=1','SSE=1 and MSE=3','SSE=−1 and MSE=−1/3','SSE=3 and MSE=3'],0,'The residuals are[1,−1,−1],their squares sum to3,and the mean divides by the three samples.',['This distinguishes sum and mean.','This reverses their normalization.','Squared errors cannot be negative.','This omits the mean’s division by sample count.'],typ='numerical',steps=['Compute residuals1,−1,−1.','Square to1,1,1and sum to3.','Divide byn=3to obtainMSE=1.'],formulas=[formula('SSE=Σ_i(y_hat_i−y_i)²; MSE=SSE/n',{'SSE':'sum of squared residuals','MSE':'mean squared residual','y_hat_i':'prediction for sample i','y_i':'true target for sample i','n':'number of samples','i':'sample index'},{'SSE,MSE':'squared target units','y_hat_i,y_i':'target units','n,i':'dimensionless'},['n>0.'])],conflict='Page795 callsMSEthe sum of squared differences;that wording describesSSEunless divided byn.')
add(795,'sse-mse-argmin','Explain why sum and mean have the same fit at fixed sample count','For a fixed positive numbern of training samples,why do minimizing SSE and minimizing MSE yield the same parameter minimizers?', ['MSE=SSE/n scales the objective by a positive parameter-independent constant','MSE andSSEalways have exactly the same numeric value','Dividing byn changes every residual’s sign but not its magnitude','The sample countn is a fitted weight that can shrink to zero'],0,'Positive constant scaling preserves the ordering of objective values and therefore the minimizing parameter set.',['This is the relevant optimization argument.','Their values differ unlessn=1orSSE=0.','Division does not reverse residual signs.','The fixed dataset’s sample count is not a fitted parameter.'],typ='derivation',steps=['WriteMSE=(1/n)SSEwithn>0fixed.','Multiplication by1/npreserves which objective values are smaller.','Conclude the minimizers agree although values differ.'])
add(795,'regression-fit-interpretation','Interpret the fitted line in noisy data','What is the purpose of the red fitted line in the regression illustration?', ['Choose model parameters that reduce the aggregate squared residuals,not necessarily pass through every point','Connect all training points in their plotting order with a piecewise path','Set every residual to zero regardless of whether the points are collinear','Assign one of two class labels according to the line’s color'],0,'A least-squares line balances residuals across the data;noisy non-collinear observations generally cannot all lie on one line.',['This is the displayed regression objective.','That would be a different interpolation construction.','A line cannot generally interpolate arbitrary non-collinear points.','The figure depicts numeric regression rather than color-based classification.'],typ='diagram')
add(795,'ols-algorithm-scope','Interpret the update-loop illustration','The diagram alternates MSE calculation and parameter updates;what can be concluded about OLS solvers in general?', ['The diagram illustrates an iterative fitting idea,butOLS can also be solved using suitable direct linear-algebra methods','EveryOLSimplementation must use this exact iterative loop and a hand-selected initial slope','OLSrequires the fitted line to rotate through every training sample in sequence','The existence of an update arrow proves the model is reinforcement learning'],0,'The objective does not uniquely specify the numerical algorithm;the diagram is not a universal implementation trace.',['This separates the squared-error criterion from its solver.','Direct least-squares methods need not follow that loop.','No such geometric requirement exists.','An optimization update does not itself imply reward-based interaction.'],typ='diagram',conflict='The page795 loop is a conceptual illustration,not evidence that every linear-regression solver uses iterative parameter initialization and updates.')
add(796,'robotics-task-table','Match grasp-success prediction to its supplied data','Which row matches the lecture’s grasp-success formulation?', ['Input point cloud plus gripper pose;output success/failure','Input depth image;output3D human-joint coordinates','Input torque and velocity time series;output actuator fault type','Input LiDAR scan features;output terrain type'],0,'The table pairs candidate grasp geometry and gripper pose with a binary success/failure target.',['This is the specified grasp row.','This is the human-pose regression row.','This is the actuator-fault row.','This is the terrain-classification row.'],typ='match')
add(796,'robotics-task-table','Identify the table’s regression output','Which listed supervised formulation predicts a continuous-coordinate output rather than an object or fault category?', ['Depth image to3D human-joint coordinates','Image pixels to object class','Torque/velocity time series to fault type','LiDAR features to terrain type'],0,'The human-pose row explicitly treats3D joint coordinates as regression targets.',['This predicts quantitative coordinates.','This predicts a category.','This predicts a fault category.','This predicts a terrain category.'],typ='match')
add(796,'robotics-task-table','Match object recognition and fault detection inputs','Which paired mapping matches the table?', ['Image pixels→object class;torque/velocity time series→fault type','Image pixels→fault type;torque/velocity time series→object class','Point cloud plus gripper pose→terrain type;LiDAR features→grasp success','Depth image→terrain label only;image pixels→all actuator torques'],0,'The table uses camera pixels for object classification and actuator time-series measurements for fault-type classification.',['This matches both listed rows.','This swaps their stated task targets.','This swaps two other table formulations.','These are not the displayed pairings.'],typ='match')
add(797,'tabular-data-scope','Qualify the rows-and-columns summary','How should the claim that everyMLdataset has samples as rows and features as columns be interpreted?', ['It describes the tabular representation used here;images,sequences and graphs can use other structured representations','It means every raw image is already stored as a two-column terrain matrix','It means supervised target labels must always be extra input-feature columns during fitting','It proves an8×8pixel array contains eight independent labeled training examples'],0,'Rows and columns are a common feature-matrix convention,not a universal native layout for every kind of data.',['This preserves the useful convention without universalizing it.','Image dimensions need not match the terrain feature count.','Targets are separate from input features in the shown supervised interface.','A wholeimagecan be one sample;its rows need not be separate labeled examples.'],conflict='Page797 says everyMLdataset has the tabular samples/features structure;this is a representation convention rather than a universal raw-data shape.')
